  "title": "Multiple sclerosis predicted effector genes (IMSGC 2019)",
  "bodyHtml": "<p>This table displays effector genes associated with multiple sclerosis, from the study:</p><p>Multiple sclerosis genomic map implicates peripheral immune cells and microglia in susceptibility.<br>International Multiple Sclerosis Genetics Consortium.<br>Science. 2019 Sep 27;365(6460):eaav7188. doi: 10.1126/science.aav7188.<br>PMID: <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31604244\" target=\"_blank\">31604244</a></p><p>An analysis of genetic data involving 47,429 individuals diagnosed with multiple sclerosis (MS) and 68,374 control subjects was conducted. This extensive study established a comprehensive genetic landscape of MS, pinpointing 200 susceptibility variants located on autosomal chromosomes outside the major histocompatibility complex (MHC), along with one variant situated on chromosome X, and 32 variants within the extended MHC region. Through the application of diverse methodologies, a total of 551 potential susceptibility genes linked to MS were prioritized, revealing their involvement in various innate and adaptive pathways spanning across different components of the immune system. The investigation also utilized expression profiles from isolated human microglia, revealing a notable presence of genes associated with MS within these immune cells present in the brain. This observation suggests a potential role for these genes in orchestrating an autoimmune response targeted towards the central nervous system. However, despite this involvement of central nervous system tissues, the findings suggest that the initial trigger for MS likely stems from disturbances in peripheral immune responses.</p><p>Mouse over the column headers for more details about each evidence type.</p>",
